Artifact Bows: 
While there are quite a few famous bows in history and mythology, the 
"Bow of Siva" (requiring great strength and dexterity to shoot) and 
Howard Hill's "Granpa" are the two most suitable for artifact status.
The former was impressive for its great range and  its ability to shoot
very heavy and therefore destructive arrows.  I think that an inexhaustible
quiver of heavy arrows should come with the"Bow of Siva."  The second 
was famous for its excellent stability.  It could hit (when used by the 
great Howard Hill) anything at less than 100 yards with a medium arrow.

Arrows:
In designing archery into nh, it seems to me that arrows should be 
divided into light, medium, and heavy.  The range of the light
arrows should be larger than the range of the heavy arrows but should 
do less damage.

Artifact Arrows:
Indian mythology is filled with artifact arrows, not bows.  These typically
had immense ranges and never missed, though they were generally one-shot
deals that could only be obtained from the gods.  Read the war books of 
the Mahabharata for details.

Regular bows:
Each bow, like each arrow, should have a range and damage.  The sum of the 
ranges should be the effective range, and the sum of the damages should 
be the effective damage.  The base to-hit probability should be 
calculated from strength and dexterity, should be constant out to 50
yards, and should decline precipitously after the effective range.  A certain
probability should be assigned to hitting things not aimed at beyond the 
effective range.

Some numbers on bow ranges (assuming properly matched arrows):
The longbow is capable of piercing armor at 150-200 yards.  The asiatic
long composite bow is capable of piercing armor at 350-450 yards.
The asiatic short composite bow was better for short range shots, say
<100yards, but it was extremely accurate at <50 yards.  The modern recurve,
usually made of wood and fiberglass, combines accurate shooting at <50 yards
with a decent range, say 180 yards.  I think that the range of nh bows
is unrealistically small, by more than a factor of 2. 

Some comments on bow lethality:
The damage an arrow does is rather like a deep stab wound.  For archers
with a moderate strength/dexterity, hits should be less frequent and more
deadly than in the current game.  For an archer with 16/16 or better, 
greater than long sword damage should be very likely at ranges < 50 yards.
Beyond 50 yards until the effective range is exceeded, dagger damage should 
be done (with a medium arrow).

Crossbows:
IT SHOULD TAKE 2 TURNS TO RELOAD AND FIRE A CROSSBOW.  It can deliver
one, short range (<50 yards) bolt very quickly, but it takes forever to reload.
Crossbow bolts are not nearly as destructive as arrows, though they are
quite accurate at the short range. 


While not all these suggestions are easy to incorporate into the game, I think
that they correctly present the rough outlines of archery as I understand them.

